    "faq_jRgYwH": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "question_eqcYiN": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What can be in it?",
            "text": "<p>We work with a number of different farms around the world, and the specific feeds vary by farm, by species, and even by the phase of the growout cycle. To meet the Seatopia Standard at Aquaculture 2.0, a feed diet includes a combination of algae oil, marine ingredients (fish meal and fish oil), high-protein plants (soy, pea, barley, corn or pistachio), essential amino acids, minerals and astaxanthin. Preventative antibiotics, hormones, GMOs and synthetic colouring agents are prohibited.</p><p>These feeds are built so the fish get the nutrition they need to produce good nutrition for humans: nutrient-dense, highly digestible, and balanced on protein, omega-3s, micronutrients and fats.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "question_8N7x6D": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What's never in it?",
            "text": "<p>Preventative antibiotics, hormones, GMOs and synthetic colouring agents are prohibited.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "question_Xiw6b8": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why is there plant protein in fish feed?",
            "text": "<p>Yes. Many of our farm partners include a percentage of high-protein, non-GMO plant meal such as soy, grains or legumes in their feed, particularly for omnivorous species or during early life stages.</p><p>Here is why. In the early days of aquaculture, what we call Aquaculture 1.0, feed was mostly fishmeal and fish oil. It was easy to produce and efficient for the fish, but it was not sustainable. Using plant proteins means less reliance on wild fishmeal while still giving fish bioavailable protein. Less wild fish in the feed also means more control over exposure to mercury, microplastics, parasites and other environmental toxins.</p><p>Seatopia exists to generate market demand for the next wave of feeds, what we call Aquaculture 3.0: species-appropriate ingredients rich in EPA and DHA, like algae, soldier fly protein and mycelium, that give fish what they need without relying on fishmeal. Some of our farms already use these ingredients and we are pushing for larger inclusions and full substitutions. Getting every partner there is the goal.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "question_UzfaND": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Are the fish dyed?",
            "text": "<p>Post-harvest and unnatural colouring agents are strictly prohibited. Colour comes from the feed, not from anything added afterwards.</p><p>Astaxanthin is the carotenoid largely responsible for a salmon’s colour. It is a naturally occurring nutrient found in kelp, krill, shrimp, lobster and crab, and it reaches the fish through what it eats. Both wild and farmed salmon get their colour from astaxanthin in their diet. It is also what makes flamingos pink.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "question_eqcYiN",
        "question_8N7x6D",
        "question_Xiw6b8",
        "question_UzfaND"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.faq.presets.name",
      "settings": {
        "style": "with-border",
        "heading": "What do the fish eat? - Fish feed FAQ",
        "heading_size": "title-md",
        "heading_alignment": "text-left md:items-end md:flex-row",
        "heading_tag": "h2",
        "subheading": "",
        "description": "<p>A fish is built from what it eats, so feed decides its omega-3s and its mineral profile, and it shapes its exposure to mercury and microplastics. That’s why we treat feed as a sourcing requirement rather than a farm detail, and why every farm we work with has to tell us what goes into the pellet. Feed varies by farm, by species, and by stage of growth.</p>",
